  <h1>Common ADEA Misconceptions</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>&ldquo;The ADEA covers all employers.&rdquo;</h2>
  <p>False. The federal ADEA applies to private employers with 20 or more employees, federal government employers, employment agencies, and labor unions. Smaller employers may be covered under state law (some states cover employers with 5 or more employees).</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;I only need to show age was a factor.&rdquo;</h2>
  <p>Not under federal law. <em>Gross v. FBL Financial Services</em> (2009) held that age must be the but-for cause of the adverse action. Showing that age was one of several factors &mdash; a mixed-motive scenario &mdash; is not enough under the ADEA.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;I can get emotional distress damages.&rdquo;</h2>
  <p>No. Unlike Title VII or the ADA, the ADEA does not provide emotional distress damages. Back pay, front pay, liquidated damages (for willful violations), and attorney fees are the remedies. Some state age discrimination laws do allow emotional distress damages.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;Age comments by coworkers prove discrimination.&rdquo;</h2>
  <p>Not automatically. Stray remarks by non-decision-makers carry limited weight. Remarks by the actual decision-maker (the person who made the hiring, firing, or promotion decision) are far more significant, especially if made close in time to the adverse action.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;Liquidated damages require proving extra harm.&rdquo;</h2>
  <p>False. Liquidated damages are automatic once willfulness is established &mdash; the plaintiff doesn&rsquo;t need to prove additional harm. The court must award them; it has no discretion once the jury finds willfulness.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;ADEA and Title VII work the same way.&rdquo;</h2>
  <p>They share some procedural similarities (EEOC exhaustion, right-to-sue letter) but differ significantly: ADEA requires but-for causation (not mixed-motive); ADEA doesn&rsquo;t allow emotional distress or punitive damages; ADEA has liquidated damages for willful violations; ADEA covers 20+ employee employers (not 15+ like Title VII).</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/how-adea-damages-work">how ADEA damages work</a>, <a href="/guides/types-of-age-discrimination">types of age discrimination</a>, and <a href="/guides/what-to-do-after-age-discrimination">what to do after age discrimination</a>.</p>
